anics are being introduced it all  kind of goes haywire so it's just mostly  just a pretty strong collaborative  process between the writers and the  designers and it all goes back to the  play testing  so the question was how sound interacts  with portal and I my memory is a little  fuzzy I'm pretty sure we tried to  replicate a little bit using just a  simple basically the exact same solution  as with our lighting where it's like we  have a sample point in front of a portal  and we have an emitter on the other side  I can't remember if we shipped that way  yeah microphone or ingame microphone and  an in-game speaker on the portals so on  both sides so if you're on this side of  the portal you'll hear what's happening  another side I pretty sure that's how we  shipped yeah but I don't think we made  any special concessions to make it seem  like it was going through a tunnel or  anything like that we just want to make  sure you could hear what's going on not  through the portal specifically I know  there's a bunch of stuff we did for like  the tractor beam like when you're into  the tractor beam it kind of puzzles like  everything else around you and like like  models the sound a little bit but going  through the portals themselves I don't  think there's anything specific we did  there are these granted I it's been a  long time I've been alone  portal  the question was what puzzles games  inspired us when we were making our game  I I can't think of a strong information  so I'm mostly a code guy so I didn't do  a whole lot of the actual like puzzle  design but when I'm thinking back we  didn't have a whole lot of sources to  draw on because it was a fairly new  concept in general yeah I can't think of  any sources I drew and I mean yeah yeah  I wasn't kidding when I said portal when  we are making tag  I think portal had come out a year or  two earlier before that and was really  good and like a big success and we were  trying to learn from how they were  teaching the players different things  and that's one of the biggest things we  took away in our student game was like  how do you teach players different  mechanics and I think portal did a  really good job of that by like teaching  by doing instead of by showing so you  like the players doing all the stuff and  then figuring it out instead of someone  showing a video or just telling the  player here's what you do next and then  going doing that so we tried to like  take that at heart and as we were doing  in our game introducing new paints and  new mechanics doing it the same way  where we introduced them in a way that  the usage is obvious enough that the  player will try it out and when they try  it out and it works they feel really  good about it  because they feel like they figured it  out instead of us doing all the work and  showing them what to do so it's like a  lot of that kind of that was the  specific thing that we took from portal  and trying to do it in our game  so the question was about how many  people it took to make portal and portal  1 portal and portal to portal 1 was a  pretty small team yes for the most part  yeah so portal 2 started as the seven  people that worked on the student game  yeah sorry portal one started with the  seven people that worked on narbacular  drop directly transplanted and then we  started working fairly early with two  writers and you know we were leveraging  other people that Valve to answer  questions and help us you know hit the  ground running as far as how to use the  engine but it was mostly just the the  nine people working day to day and then  portal 2 ballooned quite a bit portal 2  was a much bigger team by the time I  joined valve in 2009 I think there was  already a pretty big portal 2 team  they'd already had a field of mechanics  figured out the game was pretty  different from what we see now but at  that point it was already maybe 10 12  people and then like it slowly increased  like we were four of us that got hired  for our paint game so we joined that  team and then I think by the end of it  they were probably close to 40 50 people  working on it that was towards the very  end because we had to ship on to cons on  like two consoles and PC and we had a  whole co-op mechanic and there was a  whole co-op campaign being worked on  separately as well as single-player one  so it was a pretty big team by the end  of it much bigger than portal 1 and the  game itself I think is much bigger than  portal 1 as well I think the  single-player campaign itself is  probably more than twice as long  yeah I remember and in the campaign as a  co-op one is probably also that big  remember in portal 1 we would have to do  frequent integration testing and I think  I got my play time on portal 1 down to  like 45 minutes and  portal 2 is several hours yeah so it's a  much bigger game so it took a lot more  people in compared to something like  unity for both portal and portal 2 we  used source which is our in-house game  engine and it's very different from how  unity works so the way unity works is  mostly kind of like a WYSIWYG editor  like what you're seeing in the editor is  the game essentially like all the  entities and stuff are loaded obviously  more stuff gets loaded at runtime but  what you're seeing is what you're  getting whereas the way source works is  it's got a bunch of these discrete tools  that that make stuff so there's a level  editor which is where you make your  level and then you import that into the  game and you run the game separately and  what you're seeing in the level editor  is not what's what you're seeing in the  game because a lot of the entities and  stuff don't translate to the editor a  lot of the lighting and stuff has to be  pre-baked unity has some pre big  lighting as well but in the way it  worked on source source is I think it's  a combination of a bunch of like  discrete tools for each different aspect  of the game so I get a separate  particles editor a 